# Everything cargo, ruff and pytest generate. .cargo/config.toml and pyproject.toml are what put it
# here, rather than each tool's own directory at the repository root.
/build/
# The conventional location, still ignored: an explicit `--target-dir` on the command line overrides
# .cargo/config.toml, and an editor's flycheck passes one. Config chooses where output goes; this line
# is what stops a producer that ignores the config from making it committable.
/target/

# Rust
**/*.rs.bk
*.pdb
# insta's pending snapshots, accepted into the .snap beside them or discarded
*.snap.new

# Python
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pyright/
# The wheel and sdist `uv build` writes, now that there is a package to build
/dist/

# Generated data products: summation tables and profiling output
*.bin
/out/
perf.data
perf.data.old

# Every image format, rendered map or not: a figure is deployed to the site ADR 0012 rules and no image
# is a committed file here, so the rule is the format rather than the directory it was written to. This
# line stops the accident; `no-committed-images` is what refuses a `git add -f`.
*.png
*.jpg
*.jpeg
*.gif
*.webp
*.pdf
*.svg

# Local environment
.env
.DS_Store
tmp/
# GISCO domain-mask caches and their reproducible audit are local evidence, never source artifacts.
tmpg/gh-13/
# Per-developer permission allowlist. Committed here rather than left to a contributor's global
# excludes, so that `git check-ignore` gives the same answer in a clone as it does on the machine
# that created the file — which is what the lint-docs entry point relies on to tell a per-developer file
# from a broken pointer.
.claude/settings.local.json

# A partial download from `mise run data:get`. It unlinks its own on failure, but a SIGKILL leaves one
# behind and it is the size of the raster.
*.part
# The fetched raster, which `mise run data:get` is what puts there. This line keeps it out of `git
# status`, and a `git add -f` defeats it. What refuses the commit then is `check-added-large-files`, on
# size alone — the registry-row hook cannot, because the raster has a row. So nothing bounds a published
# dataset small enough to pass that hook; FU-22 holds the gap.
/data/population/*.tif
# The published settlement input, which `mise run data:get` fetches and verifies.
/data/settlements/*.geojson
# The published GISCO snapshot, which `mise run data:get` fetches and verifies.
/data/boundaries/gisco-countries-2024-1m.geojson
